<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noble Eightfold Path</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eightfold Path, Ariya Atthangika Magga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Noble Eightfold Path is an early summary of Buddhist practice leading to liberation from samsara, the cycle of rebirth, in nirvana. Its eight parts are right view, right resolve, right speech, right conduct, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness and right samadhi. It is the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition also groups it as morality, meditation and insight. The Indologist Tilmann Vetter suggests that the early description of the path may have been as simple as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a>, later elaborated into eight factors. In Buddhist symbolism the path is often shown as the eight spokes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> wheel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path.jpg" alt="Noble Eightfold Path" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Falter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dharmachakra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That path is the <b>Noble Eightfold Path</b> of right view, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness and concentration, usually grouped as ethics, meditation and wisdom.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
